    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1198-a4">

                <pb facs="0978" n="862" sameAs="frus:frus1912/pg862"/>

                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 4.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Commander Hughes to the American Consul</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1912-10-20">
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">October 20, 1912</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Mr. Canada</hi>: Both you and the captain know
                    that if active trouble arises about the discharging of the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Seguranza</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d1198-f1"/> I am the
                    one that will have the final trouble.</p>
                <p>The captain said to-day that under no circumstances would he allow arms or
                    ammunition to be landed, and I wish he would give us that assurance in writing
                    and state whether any arms or ammunition are in the cargo.</p>
                <p>If arms or ammunition are landed I shall refuse action in time of trouble. I am
                    fairly well assured that if no arms or ammunition are landed there will be no
                    trouble. I will see you in the morning.</p>
                <p>Sincerely,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">C. F. Hughes.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
